// clean-mobile-surface — the host contract.
//
// HCV-02 (Platform 16 §16.5): this file is the authoritative declaration of
// what this host provides. There is no parallel manifest. The framework
// fetches it for its Moment 1 check, `cln check` uses it at Moment 2, and the
// host compares a guest's embedded WIT against it at Moment 3 before
// instantiating.
//
// HCV-06 (§16.14): CI verifies that every interface declared here is really
// registered by this host's shell, and that nothing registered is undeclared.
// A no-op or throwing stub under a declared name is a hard failure — the
// correct response to an unimplemented interface is to omit it from this file.
//
// SCOPE: the device surface, and only the parts of it backed by an official
// Tauri v2 plugin. Bluetooth, remote push, filesystem, NFC, haptics, clipboard
// and share are omitted — the reasoning for each is in
// `system-documents/mobile-world-draft.md` §6. Omission here is not a promise
// to add them; it is HCV-06 applied.
//
// `wasi:*` comes from clean-host-core (CH-03) and is not restated — host.wit
// declares the `clean:*` surface this host owns.
//
// ---
//
// WHAT THIS HOST IS
//
// clean-mobile-surface runs a Clean guest as wasm in the WebView's engine,
// inside a Tauri Rust shell on iOS and Android. The name says where the guest
// lives: on the surface, in the page. A future peer host could run the guest
// in an embedded wasmtime instead. Both would target `wasm32-mobile` and
// implement this same `mobile` world, so switching seats is a `clean.toml`
// edit rather than a rewrite.
//
// Two rules keep that true, and every signature below obeys them:
//
// 1. COMPOSITION. This host provides the browser contract PLUS a device
//    package. It does not fork the browser surface. A guest touching no
//    device API is portable between browser and mobile unchanged; one that
//    opens the camera declares that import explicitly, and the declaration is
//    what makes it non-portable — visibly, in its own source, rather than by
//    surprise at load time.
//
//    COMPOSITION IS BY DUPLICATION, NOT BY `include`. The constraint is the
//    TRANSPORT, not WIT. `hostwit::resolve_contract` fetches exactly one URL
//    and yields one string, whose SHA-256 is pinned in `.cln/lock.toml`
//    (BVER-03); the framework then checks it textually for `world mobile`
//    (`declares_world` — "a courier, not a WIT compiler") and hands those
//    bytes to the compiler as `target_world.wit`. One file arrives, so
//    whatever this world needs must be inside it.
//
//    WIT itself would permit `include`: two files sharing
//    `package clean:host@0.1.0` resolve together and a world in one may
//    include a world in the other. That is not available here only because
//    clean-browser's file is never fetched alongside this one. And inlining
//    `world browser` into this file to make `include` legal would inherit its
//    `export` lines while still copying every interface body — the appearance
//    of reuse without the substance.
//
//    Today the duplication costs nothing: `world browser` is empty, so there
//    is nothing to copy and this file is complete as written. The cost
//    arrives with clean-browser's first real interface. The guard is
//    `tests/mobile_contract.rs`, which fails when browser declares an
//    interface this world does not carry — drift becomes a failing build
//    rather than a silent divergence. Real reuse would need a multi-file
//    contract transport, which is a framework-wide change to fetching,
//    hashing and pinning, and wants its own spec amendment.
//
// 2. VOCABULARY. Device interfaces are phrased in DEVICE terms, never browser
//    terms. `capture-photo`, not `get-user-media`. `position`, not
//    `GeolocationPosition`. A permission is a host concept, not a
//    `navigator.permissions` query. Any DOM handle, JS value, or
//    browser-lifecycle concept leaking into these signatures is precisely what
//    a native host could not honour, and closes the door this naming scheme
//    exists to hold open.
//
// ---
//
// ASYNCHRONY
//
// Device APIs are unavoidably async — the camera takes as long as the user
// takes. The shipped Clean contracts do not use `future<>` or `stream<>`:
// clean-server pairs a handle with a guest-supplied id and calls back. This
// file follows that idiom rather than introducing a second one:
//
//   - the guest calls a `request-*` function, supplying a `request-id: u32`
//     it chose
//   - the host returns `result<_, error>` immediately — did the REQUEST start,
//     not did the operation succeed
//   - on completion the host invokes the matching callback with that id
//
// The guest owns request-id allocation and must not reuse an id while its
// callback is outstanding; the host does not track liveness and would deliver
// a late result to whoever now holds the id.
//
// If WIT-native `future<>` is later adopted across all Clean hosts these
// convert mechanically. Adopting it here first would make mobile the odd host
// out.
//
// A NOTE ON WHO DECLARES WHAT. The callbacks in `world mobile` can only live
// in this hand-written file. `HostInterface::to_wit` emits exactly one bare
// interface of guest→host funcs — no world, no import — so a `frame.device`
// library's `host_bridge.cln` can declare only the outbound half. The two
// halves of one device API therefore have two owners and nothing yet checks
// they agree (mobile-world-draft.md §7, open question 2a).

package clean:host@0.1.0;

/// Permission state for a device capability.
///
/// Declared once and shared: every device interface needs it, and three
/// slightly different spellings of "denied" would be worse than one.
interface permissions {
    /// Where a capability stands with the user.
    enum state {
        /// Never asked. The guest may prompt.
        undetermined,
        /// The user allowed it.
        granted,
        /// The user refused. Asking again is usually a bad experience, and on
        /// iOS is frequently a no-op — the platform will not re-prompt.
        denied,
        /// Policy forbids it (parental controls, MDM). Not the user's choice
        /// and not recoverable by asking.
        restricted,
    }

    /// A capability a permission can be held for.
    ///
    /// One enum rather than per-interface enums, so a guest can write a single
    /// permission-handling path. Values are added only alongside the interface
    /// that needs them.
    enum capability {
        camera,
        location,
        notifications,
        biometrics,
    }

    /// Current state. Never prompts — safe to call while rendering.
    query: func(what: capability) -> state;

    /// Ask the user. Returns whether the PROMPT was raised, not the answer;
    /// the answer arrives at `permission-result`.
    ///
    /// Fails when the platform will not prompt at all — already `denied` on
    /// iOS, or `restricted` anywhere. That distinction matters: a guest that
    /// cannot tell "the user said no" from "the OS refused to ask" will loop
    /// asking forever.
    request: func(what: capability, request-id: u32) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Still-image capture (Tauri `camera` plugin).
///
/// Video and live preview streams are not declared: the plugin's supported
/// surface is capture, and a preview stream belongs to the browser surface
/// this host includes rather than to the device package.
interface camera {
    /// Which physical camera to prefer. A hint, not a guarantee — a device
    /// with no front camera honours `front` with the one it has.
    enum facing {
        front,
        rear,
    }

    /// How the capture is delivered.
    record options {
        facing: facing,
        /// JPEG quality, 1..=100. Values outside the range are clamped by the
        /// host rather than rejected: a bad quality number is never a good
        /// reason to fail a capture the user already posed for.
        quality: u8,
    }

    /// Open the capture UI. Returns when the UI has been RAISED; the image
    /// arrives at `photo-captured`.
    ///
    /// A user who cancels produces neither an error here nor a callback with
    /// data — see `photo-captured`'s docs for how cancellation is reported.
    request-photo: func(opts: options, request-id: u32) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Device position (Tauri `geolocation` plugin).
interface location {
    /// A position fix. Fields are in SI units and WGS-84, matching what both
    /// platforms report natively.
    record position {
        /// Degrees, positive north.
        latitude: f64,
        /// Degrees, positive east.
        longitude: f64,
        /// Metres above the WGS-84 ellipsoid. None when the fix is 2-D, which
        /// is common indoors — an altitude of 0 would be a lie.
        altitude: option<f64>,
        /// Horizontal 68% confidence radius, metres.
        accuracy: f64,
        /// Milliseconds since the Unix epoch, from the device clock.
        timestamp: u64,
    }

    /// How hard to work for the fix.
    enum accuracy-mode {
        /// Cell/wifi. Fast, cheap, hundreds of metres.
        coarse,
        /// GNSS. Slower, costs battery, metres.
        fine,
    }

    /// Ask for one fix. Delivered at `position-fixed`.
    request-position: func(mode: accuracy-mode, request-id: u32) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Local notifications (Tauri `notification` plugin).
///
/// LOCAL ONLY, and the boundary is deliberate. Remote push needs a server
/// credential, a device token registered with APNs/FCM, and a delivery
/// guarantee this host does not make. It is a different trust boundary and
/// belongs in a different contract, not in an extra field here.
interface notifications {
    /// What the user sees.
    record content {
        title: string,
        body: string,
        /// Guest-chosen tag. A later notification with the same tag replaces
        /// the earlier one rather than stacking — the standard way to keep a
        /// progress or status notification to one row.
        tag: option<string>,
    }

    /// Show it now. Returns once the notification is handed to the OS; there
    /// is no callback, because display is not an event the guest can act on.
    show: func(c: content) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Withdraw a previously shown notification by tag. Withdrawing a tag that
    /// is not displayed succeeds — the postcondition the guest wants is "not
    /// on screen", which is already true.
    cancel: func(tag: string) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Local user verification (Tauri `biometric` plugin).
///
/// Verification only. This interface never exposes biometric data, and never
/// will: the platform APIs do not surface it, and a contract that implied
/// otherwise would be inviting a guest to ask for something no host can give.
interface biometrics {
    /// What the device can do, as far as the guest needs to care.
    ///
    /// Deliberately not an enumeration of Face ID / Touch ID / fingerprint /
    /// iris — that is vendor vocabulary, and a guest branching on it would be
    /// making a decision the host already made better.
    enum availability {
        /// Enrolled and usable now.
        available,
        /// Hardware exists, nothing enrolled. Recoverable by the user in
        /// Settings, so worth telling them.
        not-enrolled,
        /// No hardware, or policy forbids it. Not recoverable — fall back.
        unavailable,
    }

    /// Can we verify right now?
    status: func() -> availability;

    /// Prompt for verification. The result arrives at `biometric-result`.
    ///
    /// `reason` is shown to the user by the OS and is mandatory on iOS. It is
    /// required here for both platforms so a guest cannot ship an Android
    /// build that works and an iOS build that throws at runtime.
    request-verify: func(reason: string, request-id: u32) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Application lifecycle.
///
/// A mobile app is suspended and resumed constantly, and a guest that assumes
/// otherwise loses data. Transitions are pushed to `app-state-changed` rather
/// than polled; `current` exists for the one-shot question at startup.
interface lifecycle {
    /// Where the app stands with the OS.
    enum app-state {
        /// Visible and receiving input.
        active,
        /// Visible but not focused — control centre pulled down, split screen
        /// without focus. Animations should pause; state need not be flushed.
        inactive,
        /// Not visible. Execution may be suspended at any moment, and the
        /// process may be killed without further notice.
        background,
    }

    /// The state right now.
    current: func() -> app-state;
}

/// The mobile world: the browser surface, plus devices.
///
/// The browser half is EMPTY here, and correctly so — `world browser` in
/// clean-browser is itself empty under HCV-06, so mirroring it faithfully
/// means writing nothing. When clean-browser declares its first interface, it
/// is copied in here, and `tests/mobile_contract.rs` fails until it is. See
/// the composition note in the banner for why this is duplication rather than
/// `include`.
world mobile {

    // The callbacks below name types owned by the interfaces above. WIT has
    // no dotted type paths in a world, so the names are bound with `use`.
    // This is a reference, not a second declaration: `position` here IS
    // `location`'s, which is what keeps HCV-03 true by construction rather
    // than by review.
    //
    // Note the resolved world shows `import location` / `import lifecycle`
    // that this file never wrote. `use` pulls in the defining interface, and
    // in a world that arrives as an import — so those two interfaces appear
    // in BOTH directions. Harmless (a type-only import alongside the real
    // export), but it is the kind of thing that reads as a mistake later, so:
    // it is `use` doing it, not a stray line.
    use location.{position};
    use lifecycle.{app-state};

    export permissions;
    export camera;
    export location;
    export notifications;
    export biometrics;
    export lifecycle;

    /// The user's answer to `permissions.request`.
    import permission-result: func(request-id: u32, granted: bool);

    /// A captured image, JPEG-encoded.
    ///
    /// An empty list means the user cancelled. Cancellation is not an error —
    /// it is the second normal outcome of asking someone to take a photo — and
    /// giving it its own callback would double the surface for no gain.
    import photo-captured: func(request-id: u32, image: list<u8>);

    /// A position fix. Absent when the fix could not be obtained before the
    /// host gave up.
    import position-fixed: func(request-id: u32, pos: option<position>);

    /// The outcome of `biometrics.request-verify`.
    import biometric-result: func(request-id: u32, verified: bool);

    /// Fired on every transition, not polled. A guest that must flush state
    /// before suspension cannot afford to find out by asking afterwards.
    import app-state-changed: func(to: app-state);
}
